WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.760 --> 00:00:04.280
Let's start this video by throwing a
mouse, a dog, and an elephant

00:00:04.280 --> 00:00:06.420
from a skyscraper onto something soft.

00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:08.500
Let's say, a stack of mattresses.

00:00:09.080 --> 00:00:11.360
The mouse lands and is stunned for a moment,

00:00:11.360 --> 00:00:12.620
before it shakes itself off,

00:00:12.620 --> 00:00:14.200
and walks away pretty annoyed,

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:15.900
because that's a very rude thing to do.

00:00:16.500 --> 00:00:18.000
The dog breaks all of its bones

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:20.260
and dies in an unspectacular way,

00:00:20.260 --> 00:00:24.060
and the elephant explodes into a red puddle of bones and insides

00:00:24.060 --> 00:00:25.800
and has no chance to
be annoyed.

00:00:26.340 --> 00:00:27.860
Why does the mouse survive,

00:00:27.860 --> 00:00:29.780
but the elephant and dog don't?

00:00:30.640 --> 00:00:32.040
The answer is size.

00:00:32.880 --> 00:00:36.700
Size is the most underappreciated 
regulator of living things.

00:00:36.700 --> 00:00:39.200
Size determines everything about our biology,

00:00:39.200 --> 00:00:43.060
how we are built, how we
experience the world, how we live and die.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:47.740
It does so because the physical laws are
different for different sized animals.

00:00:47.750 --> 00:00:54.059
Life spans seven orders of magnitude,
from invisible bacteria to mites, ants,

00:00:54.059 --> 00:01:00.870
mice, dogs, humans, elephants and, blue
whales. Every size lives in its own

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:05.670
unique universe right next to each other,
each with its own rules, upsides, and

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:11.400
downsides. We'll explore these different
worlds in a series of videos. Let's get

00:01:11.400 --> 00:01:17.610
back to the initial question: Why did our
mouse survive the fall? Because of how

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:21.659
scaling size changes everything; a principle that we'll meet over and

00:01:21.659 --> 00:01:26.580
over again. Very small things, for example,
are practically immune to falling from

00:01:26.580 --> 00:01:30.000
great heights because the smaller you
are the less you care about the effect

00:01:30.000 --> 00:01:34.040
of gravity.
Imagine a theoretical spherical animal

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:38.780
the size of a marble. It has three
features: its length, its surface area,

00:01:38.780 --> 00:01:43.700
(which is covered in skin) and its volume,
or all the stuff inside it like organs,

00:01:43.700 --> 00:01:49.490
muscles, hopes and dreams. If we make it
ten times longer, say the size of a

00:01:49.490 --> 00:01:54.170
basketball, the rest of its features
don't just grow ten times. Its skin will

00:01:54.170 --> 00:02:01.250
grow 100 times and it's inside (so it's
volume) grows by 1000 times. The volume

00:02:01.250 --> 00:02:06.380
determines the weight, or more accurately,
mass of the animal. The more mass you

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:09.560
have, the higher your kinetic energy
before you hit the ground and the

00:02:09.560 --> 00:02:14.180
stronger the impact shock. The more
surface area in relation to your volume

00:02:14.180 --> 00:02:18.590
or mass you have, the more the impact
gets distributed and softened, and also

00:02:18.590 --> 00:02:23.180
the more air resistance will slow you
down. An elephant is so big that it has

00:02:23.180 --> 00:02:27.590
extremely little surface area in ratio
to its volume. So a lot of kinetic energy

00:02:27.590 --> 00:02:31.610
gets distributed over a small space and
the air doesn't slow it down much at all.

00:02:31.610 --> 00:02:36.260
That's why it's completely destroyed in
an impressive explosion of goo when it

00:02:36.260 --> 00:02:41.360
hits the ground. The other extreme,
insects, have a huge surface area in

00:02:41.360 --> 00:02:44.930
relation to their tiny mass so you can
literally throw an ant from an airplane

00:02:44.930 --> 00:02:49.970
and it will not be seriously harmed. But
while falling is irrelevant in the small

00:02:49.970 --> 00:02:53.630
world there are other forces for the
harmless for us but extremely dangerous

00:02:53.630 --> 00:02:58.940
for small beings. Like surface tension
which turns water into a potentially

00:02:58.940 --> 00:03:04.190
deadly substance for insects. How does it
work? Water has the tendency to stick to

00:03:04.190 --> 00:03:08.120
itself; its molecules are attracted to
each other through a force called

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.170
cohesion which creates a tension on its
surface that you can imagine as a sort

00:03:12.170 --> 00:03:17.180
of invisible skin. For us this skin is so
weak that we don't even notice it

00:03:17.180 --> 00:03:22.190
normally. If you get wet about 800 grams
of water or about one percent of your

00:03:22.190 --> 00:03:26.810
body weight sticks to you. A wet mouse
has about 3 grams of water sticking to

00:03:26.810 --> 00:03:31.850
it, which is more than 10% of its body
weight. Imagine having eight full water

00:03:31.850 --> 00:03:36.260
bottle sticking to you when you leave
the shower. But for an insect the force

00:03:36.260 --> 00:03:40.340
of water surface tension is so strong
that getting wet is a question of life

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:43.480
and death.
If we were to shrink you to the size of

00:03:43.480 --> 00:03:47.710
an ant and you touch water it would be
like you were reaching into glue. It

00:03:47.710 --> 00:03:51.430
would quickly engulf you, its surface
tension too hard for you to break and

00:03:51.430 --> 00:03:58.120
you'd drown. So insects evolved to be water
repellent. For one their exoskeleton is

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:02.770
covered with a thin layer of wax just
like a car. This makes their surface at

00:04:02.770 --> 00:04:07.750
least partly water repellent because it
can't stick to it very well. Many insects

00:04:07.750 --> 00:04:12.640
are also covered with tiny hairs that
serve as a barrier. They vastly increase

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.180
their surface area and prevent the
droplets from touching their exoskeleton

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:20.739
and make it easier to get rid of
droplets. To make use of surface tension

00:04:20.739 --> 00:04:26.290
evolution cracked nanotechnology
billions of years before us. Some insects

00:04:26.290 --> 00:04:30.220
have evolved a surface covered by a
short and extremely dense coat of water

00:04:30.220 --> 00:04:35.770
repelling hair. Some have more than a
million hairs per square millimeter when

00:04:35.770 --> 00:04:40.120
the insect dives under water air stays
inside their fur and forms a coat of air.

00:04:40.120 --> 00:04:44.350
Water can't enter it because their hairs are
too tiny to break its surface tension.

00:04:44.350 --> 00:04:49.780
But it gets even better, as the oxygen of
the air bubble runs out, new oxygen

00:04:49.780 --> 00:04:52.870
diffuses into the bubble from the water
around, it while the carbon dioxide

00:04:52.870 --> 00:04:58.390
diffuses outwards into the water. And so
the insect carries its own outside lung

00:04:58.390 --> 00:05:01.860
around and can basically breathe
underwater thanks to surface tension.

00:05:01.860 --> 00:05:06.280
This is the same principle that enables
pond skaters to walk on water by the way,

00:05:06.280 --> 00:05:12.730
tiny anti-water hairs. The smaller you get
the weirder the environment becomes. At

00:05:12.730 --> 00:05:17.770
some point even air becomes more and
more solid. Let's now zoom down to the

00:05:17.770 --> 00:05:21.040
smallest insects known, about half the size
of a grain of salt,

00:05:21.040 --> 00:05:27.580
only 0.15 millimeters long: the Fairy Fly.
They live in a world even weirder than

00:05:27.580 --> 00:05:32.650
another insects. For them air itself
is like thin jello, a syrup-like mass

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:37.330
surrounding them at all times.
Movement through it is not easy. Flying

00:05:37.330 --> 00:05:41.290
on this level is not like elegant
gliding; they have to kind of grab and

00:05:41.290 --> 00:05:46.510
hold onto air. So their wings look like
big hairy arms rather than proper insect

00:05:46.510 --> 00:05:51.340
wings. They literally swim through the
air, like a tiny gross alien through

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:55.100
syrup.
Things only become stranger from here on

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:59.780
as we explore more diversity of
different sizes. The physical rules are

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:03.560
so different for each size that
evolution had to engineer around them

00:06:03.560 --> 00:06:08.900
over and over as life grew in size in
the last billion years. So why are there

00:06:08.900 --> 00:06:14.960
no ants the size of horses?
Why are no elephants the size of amoeba? Why?

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:17.440
We'll discuss this in the next part.

